"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Kids spit out certain foods and turned down others."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use to talk about eating habits."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to clean up"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to completely finish your plate "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Parents want their children to clean up their plates."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to give in "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " to surrender; to admit defeat "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Parents eventually gave in and let the child eat whatever they wanted."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to eat up"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to finish everything "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Video showed role models eating up food the kids disliked."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "compelling "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "exciting; very interesting"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: A compelling study in Wales shows that fussy kids can be convinced to eat."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "thrilled"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "excited "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Parents everywhere are thrilled."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tactic"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " strategy "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Parents used to try different tactics to get their children to eat."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud07"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "fussy"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "difficult to please "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Fussy children can be convinced to eat their food."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le08ob06aud08"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adjectives About Feelings and Reactions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You talk about feelings and reactions using adjectives.  It is important to use the correct kind of adjective - it is common for English language learners to confuse the two kinds of adjectives when talking about feelings and reactions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ol><li><span class=\"strong\">-ed</span> adjectives<br/><br/>Adjectives that end in <span class=\"strong\"> -ed </span> are used to describe how a person <span class=\"strong\">feels </span>, like <span class=\"italic\">annoyed </span>, <span class=\"italic\">fascinated </span>, <span class=\"italic\">entertained </span>, <span class=\"italic\">surprised </span>, <span class=\"italic\">worried </span> or <span class=\"italic\">tired</span>.<br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">I am annoyed.<br/> Parents are thrilled.</span><br/><br/><li><span class=\"strong\"> -ing</span> adjectives</li><br/>Adjectives that end in <span class=\"strong\">-ing </span> are used to describe things and situations, like <span class=\"italic\">annoying </span>, <span class=\"italic\"> entertaining</span>, <span class=\"italic\"> surprising</span>, <span class=\"italic\"> worrying</span> or <span class=\"italic\">tiring</span>.<br/> These adjectives describe the <span class=\u201dstrong\u201d>effect</span> something has on a person.<br/><br/><span class=\"italic\">The study is compelling. </span>(It makes me feel compelled). <br/><span class=\"italic\">Work is tiring.</span> (It makes me feel tired).</li></ol>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Now a compelling study in Wales has shown that fussy kids can be convinced to eat healthy foods."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Parents everywhere are thrilled!"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Used to and Would"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> and <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> have similar meanings. They are both used to describe something that happened regularly in the past but doesn't happen any longer. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span> to tell stories and share anecdotes about the past."}, {"type": "text", "content": "However, there is an important difference between <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> and would<span class=\"strong\"></span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> is not used unless you know that the time frame is in the past, while <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> does not require this. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read the following example: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">We would visit India every summer when we were children.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">When we were children</span> refers to a past time frame and therefore makes the meaning of <span class=\"italic\">would</span> clear."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">We used to visit India every summer when we were children.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Here, you do not have to include a past time frame, since the meaning of <span class=\"italic\">used to</span> is clear."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn7le08ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Choose a person to interview about their current or past eating habits."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Ask them to describe their eating habits and if their parents ever tried any tactics to get them to try out new foods."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write down a short summary of what they said."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Try to use adjectives, phrasal verbs and <span class=\"italic\">used to</span>/<span class=\"italic\">would</span>."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I interview someone about their eating habits."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe people and things."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about past and present habits."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "img_path": "", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can discuss eating habits. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "  I can talk about habits in the past."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can describe people and things. "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " Do you have good eating habits?  Why or why not?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " What were your parents' eating habits like when you were growing up? "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " Did you use to be a picky eater?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": 